13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 10:30 a.m.–2 p.m.","place":"Locust Grove Run, the peach orchard, Duncan Field, Davis Wheat Field, and the farm lane later called the Sunken Road at Shiloh, Tennessee","result":"Stuart’s isolated brigade held near the river for about two hours despite being outnumbered, while MacArthur’s brigade absorbed part of Jackson’s attack. The Wallace–Prentiss–Hurlbut line used woods, thickets, and the farm lane to stop several separate Confederate assaults. Shaver withdrew after first contact at Duncan Field; Cheatham’s attack failed at about 11 a.m.; Hindman’s larger attack lost coordination before noon; and Gibson’s brigade made repeated unsuccessful attacks until it was exhausted by about 2 p.m. The position later called the Hornet’s Nest held because the Confederate center remained thin and the attacks arrived separately","facts":[{"label":"Stuart’s initial opposition","value":"Three United States regiments against nine Confederate regiments"},{"label":"Stuart’s remaining force","value":"About 800 after the first withdrawal"},{"label":"9th Illinois casualties","value":"361 killed or wounded; 62 percent of the regiment"},{"label":"Confederate distribution at 11 a.m.","value":"Eight brigades west, two east, one in the center, four in reserve or moving"},{"label":"Cheatham–Stevens force","value":"About 1,000 men"},{"label":"Hindman’s assault","value":"About 3,700 men from parts of four brigades"},{"label":"Hindman’s advance","value":"About 80 yards into Duncan Field before retreating"},{"label":"Gibson’s attacks","value":"Three or four assaults; brigade exhausted by about 2 p.m."}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map identifies Locust Grove Run, the peach orchard, Duncan Field, the Hornet’s Nest area, the road network, and Pittsburg Landing.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"United States left and Confederate attacks","caption":"Stuart and MacArthur held east of the main Wallace–Prentiss–Hurlbut line. Separate Confederate forces attacked through Duncan Field, Davis Wheat Field, and the woods between them rather than striking together.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-hornets-nest-noon.svg","alt":"Map of Confederate attacks on the United States left and Hornet’s Nest at Shiloh"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the farm lane, fields, roads, camps, and troop positions across the battlefield.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"7:14","audioTime":434,"title":"The episode returns to Stuart’s isolated brigade","detail":"One Illinois and two Ohio regiments guarded the far United States left near the Tennessee River."},{"time":"8:35","audioTime":515,"title":"Chalmers and Jackson reached Stuart","detail":"At about 11 a.m., nine Confederate regiments and two batteries deployed against Stuart’s three regiments behind Locust Grove Run."},{"time":"9:36","audioTime":576,"title":"The 71st Ohio broke","detail":"Colonel Rodney Mason fled, part of the regiment followed, and the unit disintegrated after its second-in-command was mortally wounded."},{"time":"10:05","audioTime":605,"title":"Stuart rebuilt a shorter line","detail":"The 55th Illinois rallied about 200 yards back and joined the 54th Ohio and part of the 71st Ohio, leaving about 800 men with a ravine behind them."},{"time":"11:11","audioTime":671,"title":"The Confederate attack slowed","detail":"Chalmers’s men needed ammunition, soldiers left the ranks for captured camps, and Stuart’s reduced line continued firing."},{"time":"12:15","audioTime":735,"title":"MacArthur drew off part of Jackson’s brigade","detail":"Three Illinois regiments held about a quarter mile west of Stuart. The 9th Illinois lost 361 killed and wounded, 62 percent of its strength."},{"time":"14:31","audioTime":871,"title":"The entire United States line is identified","detail":"From east to west it ran through Stuart, MacArthur, Hurlbut, Prentiss, W. H. L. Wallace, then the retreating McClernand and Sherman."},{"time":"15:37","audioTime":937,"title":"The Confederate center was nearly empty","detail":"At about 11 a.m., eight brigades faced the United States right and two attacked Stuart, while only one faced Hurlbut, Prentiss, and Wallace."},{"time":"16:24","audioTime":984,"title":"Bragg and Polk improvised a new command arrangement","detail":"With corps intermingled, Bragg took the right, Polk the center, and Hardee the left. Johnston was acting locally near the front."},{"time":"18:18","audioTime":1098,"title":"Shaver made first contact at the Hornet’s Nest","detail":"At about 10:30 a.m., his Arkansas brigade entered Duncan Field and withdrew after Tuttle’s Iowans opened fire."},{"time":"19:35","audioTime":1175,"title":"Stevens’s brigade made the first organized assault","detail":"At about 11 a.m., Benjamin Cheatham led roughly 1,000 men toward Wallace’s position after Stevens was injured in a fall."},{"time":"21:30","audioTime":1290,"title":"The Iowans fired at thirty yards","detail":"The 12th and 14th Iowa delivered two close volleys, then William Shaw led a short counterattack before returning to the farm lane."},{"time":"22:24","audioTime":1344,"title":"Cheatham’s brigade withdrew with heavy losses","detail":"All twelve members of the 6th Tennessee color guard fell; the regiment lost fourteen officers and 226 enlisted men."},{"time":"23:02","audioTime":1382,"title":"Grant ordered the line held","detail":"Around 11 a.m., he visited Hurlbut, Prentiss, and Wallace. Prentiss understood the order to hold at all hazards literally."},{"time":"23:45","audioTime":1425,"title":"Hindman assembled the largest assault","detail":"About 3,700 men from parts of four brigades advanced around 11:30 with artillery support."},{"time":"25:24","audioTime":1524,"title":"Hindman’s attack lost command and momentum","detail":"Hindman was disabled when his horse was hit, Shaver had no horse, Wood was already injured, and the attacking formations withdrew after moving about 80 yards."},{"time":"26:23","audioTime":1583,"title":"Two organized attacks had failed by noon","detail":"The United States center had not yet faced the kind of combined pressure that broke Sherman and McClernand."},{"time":"27:27","audioTime":1647,"title":"Bragg sent Gibson’s brigade forward","detail":"One Arkansas and three Louisiana regiments advanced across Davis Wheat Field shortly after noon."},{"time":"28:04","audioTime":1684,"title":"United States infantry fired from the thicket","detail":"The defenders waited until Gibson’s men were only yards away, then delivered a point-blank volley from concealed positions."},{"time":"29:21","audioTime":1761,"title":"A bayonet charge failed in the brush","detail":"The 19th Louisiana entered the thicket but could not move through it and withdrew under fire."},{"time":"30:05","audioTime":1805,"title":"Gibson renewed the attack several times","detail":"Accounts differed between three and four assaults. Each was repulsed, and the brigade was exhausted by about 2 p.m."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Visited the center and ordered Prentiss to hold his position"},{"commander":"W. H. L. Wallace","command":"2nd Division","subordinates":"Tuttle and Sweeney along the farm lane; MacArthur detached toward the river"},{"commander":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","command":"Remnant of the 6th Division","subordinates":"Held between Wallace and Hurlbut under Grant’s order"},{"commander":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Two brigades from the farm lane through the peach orchard"},{"commander":"John McArthur","command":"Detached brigade, Wallace’s division","subordinates":"Three Illinois regiments between Hurlbut and Stuart"},{"commander":"David Stuart","command":"Detached brigade, Sherman’s division","subordinates":"54th and 71st Ohio, 55th Illinois near Locust Grove Run"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Braxton Bragg","command":"Ad hoc right-wing command","subordinates":"Directed later attacks against the Hornet’s Nest, including Gibson’s brigade"},{"commander":"Leonidas Polk","command":"Ad hoc center command","subordinates":"Tried to restore control after the corps became intermingled"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Ad hoc left-wing command","subordinates":"Assigned the western battlefield under the improvised arrangement"},{"commander":"James R. Chalmers / John K. Jackson","command":"Brigades on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Nine regiments attacking Stuart and MacArthur"},{"commander":"Benjamin F. Cheatham / William H. Stephens","command":"First organized Hornet’s Nest assault","subordinates":"One Kentucky and two Tennessee regiments, about 1,000 men"},{"commander":"Thomas C. Hindman / Alexander P. Stewart","command":"Combined 11:30 a.m. assault","subordinates":"About 3,700 men from parts of four brigades"},{"commander":"Randall L. Gibson","command":"Brigade","subordinates":"One Arkansas and three Louisiana regiments in repeated afternoon attacks"}]}],"people":[{"name":"W. H. L. Wallace","role":"Commanded the principal United States force along the farm lane at the Hornet’s Nest","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","role":"Held the center under Grant’s order to maintain the position at all hazards","side":"United States"},{"name":"David Stuart","role":"Re-formed his heavily outnumbered brigade and held near the river for about two hours","side":"United States"},{"name":"John McArthur","role":"Commanded three Illinois regiments that drew part of Jackson’s attack away from Stuart","side":"United States"},{"name":"James M. Tuttle","role":"Commanded the Iowa brigade that stopped the first attacks across Duncan Field","side":"United States"},{"name":"Andrew Hickenlooper","role":"Commanded the 5th Ohio Battery supporting the Hornet’s Nest defense","side":"United States"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Took tactical direction of the Confederate right and ordered Gibson’s repeated attacks","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670042/"},{"name":"Thomas C. Hindman","role":"Assembled the largest assault against the center but was disabled when artillery struck his horse","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Randall L. Gibson","role":"Led three or four unsuccessful attacks through Davis Wheat Field and dense brush","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Benjamin F. Cheatham","role":"Led Stephens’s brigade after Stephens was injured and was slightly wounded in the failed assault","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brigadier General Thomas C. Hindman","side":"Confederacy","role":"Organizer of the 11:30 a.m. combined assault","outcome":"Disabled when a United States cannonball struck his horse and the concussion or fall removed him from the fight"},{"name":"Brigadier General Sterling A. M. Wood","side":"Confederacy","role":"Brigade commander in Hindman’s combined force","outcome":"Removed from the fight after friendly fire wounded his horse, which threw and dragged him"},{"name":"Major General Benjamin F. Cheatham","side":"Confederacy","role":"Division commander leading Stephens’s brigade","outcome":"Slightly wounded during the failed attack across Duncan Field"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"Open fields gave way to dense woods and thickets that concealed defenders and disrupted attacking formations.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"The Hornet’s Nest fighting consisted of separate attacks by different Confederate formations between late morning and mid-afternoon.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"},{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"The center’s resistance helped cover the route back toward the United States supply base on the Tennessee River.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 118","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/118-shiloh-part-the-eighth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Wallace’s 2nd Division","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=H"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"}]}}]}]
